#Objective
•	To implement a secure sudo policy in order to :
•	control administrative privileges,
•	limit abuse,
•	strengthen traceability, and
•	apply the principle of least privilege.


#Context
In professional environments :
•	not all users should have full privileges,
•	administrative actions must be controlled,
•	sensitive commands must be restricted.
System administrators therefore use :
•	sudo groups,
•	specific sudo rules,
•	sudo logs,
•	command restrictions.


#Policy Security
| Policy                | Value    |
| --------------------- | -------- |
| Authorized Admin User | gadmin   |
| Root Direct Login     | Disabled |
| Sudo Logging          | Enabled  |
| Least Privilege       | Applied  |


#Verify Sudo Access
groups gadmin
sudo whoami


#Create Security Group
sudo groupadd securityadmins
sudo usermod -aG securityadmins gadmin
groups gadmin


#Configure Custom Sudo Policy
sudo visudo -f /etc/sudoers.d/securityadmins
%securityadmins ALL=(ALL) /usr/bin/systemctl, /usr/bin/journalctl


#Explanation
| Component       | Purpose                   |
| --------------- | -----------------------   |
| %securityadmins | Authorized group          |
| ALL=(ALL)       | Every hosts/users         |
| systemctl       | Services management       |
| journalctl      | Logs consultation         |


#Validate Sudo Syntax
sudo visudo -c
sudo visudo -c -f /etc/sudoers.d/securityadmins


#Test Sudo Policy
sudo systemctl status ssh --no-pager
sudo journalctl -n 5
sudo apt update


#Sudo Logging
sudo tail -f /var/log/auth.log

